  "narrator": "Mr. Ginting",
  "desc": "A calm bedtime Bible story about tired Elijah, God’s care and His gentle whisper, with a lullaby and a short prayer.",
  "seo_title": "Elijah and the Still Small Voice | Bedtime Bible Story for Kids #2",
  "seconds": 257.98,
  "minutes": "4:18",
  "story": "Hi, little light… It’s bedtime story time. Are you all snuggled in? Pull your blanket up, nice and cozy. Tonight’s story is about a man who was very, very tired… and about how God took care of him. It’s called… Elijah and the Still Small Voice.\n\nA long time ago, there lived a man named Elijah. Elijah loved God, and he had done many brave things for Him. But one day, Elijah felt tired. Not just sleepy-tired… but tired all the way down inside. He walked a long, long way into the desert, all by himself. He sat down under a little tree, and he said, “God, I’m so tired. I can’t do this anymore.” And then… Elijah lay down, and he fell fast asleep.\n\nBut God had not forgotten Elijah. Not for one second. While Elijah was sleeping, an angel came and gently touched him. “Get up and eat,” the angel said. And there, right beside him, was warm bread, baked on hot stones… and a jar of cool, fresh water. Elijah ate, and he drank, and he lay down to sleep again. Then the angel came a second time. “Eat some more,” he said kindly. “You have a long journey ahead.” God knew Elijah needed food. And God knew Elijah needed rest. God knows what you need, too.\n\nWith his tummy full and his body rested, Elijah walked for many days and many nights, until he came to a great mountain. He found a little cave, and he went inside to sleep. Then God said, “Go out and stand on the mountain.” So Elijah stood at the opening of the cave… and waited. First, there came a big, strong wind. Whoosh… whoosh… It blew so hard that rocks tumbled down the mountain. But God was not in the wind. Then the ground began to shake… rumble, rumble. But God was not in the shaking. Then there was a fire, far away, glowing orange in the dark. But God was not in the fire.\n\nAnd then… everything became quiet. So quiet. And the Bible says, “After the fire, there was a still small voice.” A gentle whisper. Elijah pulled his cloak over his face, and he stepped outside… and God spoke to him, quietly and kindly. God told Elijah that he was not alone. God still had good work for him to do… and God gave him a friend, named Elisha, to help him.\n\nYou know, little one… God doesn’t always speak in big, loud ways. Sometimes, He speaks in the quiet. When you’re lying in bed, and the house is still… that’s a good time to talk to God. And to listen. Here’s a verse for sleepy time: “In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you alone, the LORD, make me live in safety.”",
  "prayer": "Dear God… thank You for taking care of me, just like You took care of Elijah. When I’m tired, help me rest. When it’s quiet, help me hear You. Amen.",
  "goodnight": "Goodnight, little light. Sleep tight. 🌙",
  "verse": "In peace I will both lay myself down and sleep, for you alone, the LORD, make me live in safety.",
  "ref": "Psalm 4:8",
